{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And you shall overlay it with pure gold, inside and out you shall overlay it.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory darshaned from here concerning a Torah scholar, that his inside should be like his outside, just as the Ark was, which was gold on the inside and on the outside. And Scripture hinted at this when it said (Job 29:14): ״צדק לבשתי וילבשני״ — 'Righteousness I put on, and it clothed me' — meaning, inside and outside. And our Rabbis of blessed memory darshaned: why were the words of Torah likened to glass (זכוכית), as it says (ibid. v. 17) ״לא יערכנה זהב וזכוכית״? To tell you: just as glass is clear and shows you everything that is inside it, so a Torah scholar is worthy that his inside be like his outside." "<b>And you shall make upon it a crown of gold round about.</b> In the case of the Table and the altar of incense you find ״ועשית לו״ — 'you shall make for it' — but here, with the Ark, ״ועשית עליו״ — 'you shall make upon it.' The reason for this is that the Table alluded to the crown of kingship and the altar of incense alluded to the crown of priesthood, and kingship and priesthood are a man's inheritance: for kingship in Israel belongs only to one who is of the seed of David, and priesthood only to one who is of the seed of Aaron — as it is written concerning kingship (Deuteronomy 17:18-20): ״וכתב לו את משנה התורה״… ״למען יאריך ימים על ממלכתו״, and it is written concerning priesthood: ״והיתה לו ולזרעו אחריו״. But with the Ark, which alludes to Torah — and Torah is not a man's inheritance — therefore it is written ״ועשית עליו״ and it did not say ״ועשית לו״, for it is not an inheritance for him in the manner of kingship and priesthood." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״ועשית עליו״ — 'upon it' is mentioned regarding the Ark, whereas regarding the Table and the altar it is not so, to teach you that the crown of Torah is supreme above all. If a person merited Torah, it is as if he merited all of them, as it says: ״זר זהב סביב״. And זר is written without a י״ו״ד, to teach you: if a person merited Torah, it becomes for him a זר (crown); if he did not merit Torah, it becomes for him זר (alien/stranger). Another interpretation: if a person merited Torah — that he learns and fulfills it — behold he is ״זר זהב״ (a crown of gold); and if not, it becomes זרה (alienated) from him, that is to say, it withdraws from him and does not join with him. Therefore it is written זר without a י״ו״ד, in the language of זרה (estrangement)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "He cites Job 28:17 for the glass comparison; the chapter reference in the first citation (29:14) is correct for the 'righteousness' verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:14.898633839Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}